<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Regensburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1245 CE – 1803 CE · capital Regensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Danube city of Roman origin, a great medieval trading hub and from 1663 the permanent seat of the Imperial Diet of the Holy Roman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Regensburg grew from the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castra-regina">Castra Regina</a> and became the seat of the Bavarian dukes and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Its merchants traded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and the Stone Bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was built in the 1130s and 1140s. In 1245 Frederick II granted the city self-government, making it an imperial city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Patrician merchant families, whose tower houses still stand, ruled through the council. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, several imperial abbeys and the city shared the town. Trade declined in the 15th century, and Regensburg briefly submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1486 to 1492. It adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1542. From 1663 to 1806 it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Perpetual Imperial Diet</a>, the Empire's standing assembly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the city became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-regensburg">Principality of Regensburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>, and in 1810 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. Its medieval old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1245 CE – 1803 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.48%;width:11.10%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1245 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Regensburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regensburg, Ratisbon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1524&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-regensburg" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Regensburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4959 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Regensburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5279 723L5229 694M5279 723L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Regensburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1524&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
